    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "a735696c_a98fd25b",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1137063
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-07-02T05:43:51Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Samsung is adding members to this struct in their changes for SDP and DualDAR.\n\nThere are a few possible ways to solve the KMI mismatch (which occurs in the CRCs of functions in files that include \u003clinux/fscrypt.h\u003e and export KMI functions that have a struct dentry/inode/file parameter, e.g. fs/dcache.c):\n\n1. Add some ANDROID_VENDOR_DATA() members to struct fscrypt_operations\n2. Use #ifdefs to hide the definition of fscrypt_operations from the problematic files\n3. Same as (2) but put the fscrypt_operations in a separate header\n\nWe already went through this last year, and people preferred (2), which was submitted to android11-5.4.  It could be done one of the other ways, but as far as I know nothing has changed about the problem itself.\n\nI don\u0027t see a way to upstream any of these workarounds.  Putting struct fscrypt_operations in a separate header would come closest, but I just don\u0027t see a convincing reason for it...  It would still fundamentally just be a workaround to keep the CRCs of certain symbols from changing.  I\u0027m happy to consider any ideas you have though...",
      "parentUuid": "7afa9ef8_d47f71e1",
      "revId": "5a0147f1712707dc49b97a3f872fd9fc2fbeee8a",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
